# Coding Patterns to Revise

Patterns observed in the OS codebase worth studying in depth.

---

## 1. Newtype Pattern for Type Safety

`vga_buffer.rs:31`, `task/mod.rs:29`

```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
struct ColorCode(u8);

struct TaskId(u64);
```

Wrapping a primitive in a struct to make it a distinct type. `ColorCode` and `u8` are the same
in memory, but the compiler treats them as different — you can't pass a raw `u8` where a
`ColorCode` is expected. Good pattern to prevent mixing up unrelated integers.

---

## 2. Interior Mutability via a Custom Locked Wrapper

`allocator.rs:17-31`

```rust
pub struct Locked<A> {
    inner: spin::Mutex<A>,
}
```

`GlobalAlloc::alloc` takes `&self` (shared reference), but allocating requires mutation.
`Locked<A>` wraps a spinlock so mutation is allowed through a shared reference. This is the
standard interior mutability pattern, here rolled manually instead of using `RefCell` (not `Sync`)
or `std::Mutex` (unavailable). Understand why `GlobalAlloc` requires `&self` and why that forces
this design.

---

## 3. `unsafe impl` for Safety Contracts You Uphold Manually

`memory.rs:13`, `allocator/bump.rs:28`

```rust
unsafe impl FrameAllocator<Size4KiB> for BootInfoFrameAllocator { ... }
unsafe impl GlobalAlloc for Locked<BumpAllocator> { ... }
```

These traits are `unsafe` because the compiler can't verify correctness — you're promising "I
guarantee all `USABLE` frames in the memory map are actually unused" or "my allocator won't return
overlapping regions." Study what invariants each `unsafe trait` requires; the `unsafe impl` is
your explicit commitment to upholding them.

