## 4. Split Borrow via Struct Destructuring

`task/executor.rs:32-36`

```rust
fn run_ready_tasks(&mut self) {
    let Self { tasks, task_queue, waker_cache } = self;
    // now borrow tasks, task_queue, waker_cache independently
```

Rust's borrow checker tracks borrows per field when you destructure. Without this,
`self.tasks.get_mut(...)` and `self.task_queue.clone()` in the same scope would both be `&mut
self` borrows and conflict. Destructuring splits them into separate bindings so the borrow checker
allows both simultaneously.

