## 9. Race-Condition-Free Waker Registration

`task/keyboard.rs:42-53`

```rust
fn poll_next(self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context) -> Poll<Option<u8>> {
    let queue = SCANCODE_QUEUE.try_get().expect("Not initialized");
    if let Some(scancode) = queue.pop() {
        return Poll::Ready(Some(scancode));
    }
    WAKER.register(&cx.waker());   // register BEFORE second check
    match queue.pop() {
        Some(scancode) => { WAKER.take(); Poll::Ready(Some(scancode)) }
        None => Poll::Pending,
    }
}
```

The double-check is intentional. Without the second `queue.pop()` after registering the waker,
there's a race: interrupt fires between the first `pop` (returns None) and `register`, pushing a
scancode and calling `wake()` — but no waker is registered yet, so the task sleeps forever.
Registering first, then checking again closes the window. This is a classic async pattern.

