## 11. `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>` for Heap-Allocated Futures

`task/mod.rs:11`

```rust
future: Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = ()>>>,
```

`Pin` prevents the future from being moved in memory after it starts executing (self-referential
futures would break if moved). `Box` heap-allocates it so the `Task` struct has a fixed size
regardless of how large the concrete future is. `dyn Future` makes it a trait object so different
future types can be stored in the same collection. All three are needed together for a practical
executor.

---

## 12. `OnceCell` for Interrupt-Safe One-Time Initialization

`task/keyboard.rs:12`

```rust
static SCANCODE_QUEUE: OnceCell<ArrayQueue<u8>> = OnceCell::uninit();
```

The queue can't be initialized at compile time (it needs heap allocation), but it also can't use
`lazy_static!` here because the interrupt handler calls `try_get()` — if initialization panicked,
`lazy_static` would deadlock. `OnceCell::try_init_once` initializes exactly once and `try_get`
returns an error rather than blocking if uninitialized. Used where the "not yet initialized" case
must be handled explicitly rather than panicked on.

---

## Priority Order for Revision

1. Interior mutability (`Locked<A>`, `RefCell`, `Mutex`) — foundational, appears everywhere
2. `unsafe impl` contracts — critical for systems code; know what you're promising
3. Split borrow via destructuring — saves you from fighting the borrow checker
4. `impl Trait` returns — used constantly in iterator chains
5. `Pin` + `Box<dyn Future>` — essential for async runtime code
6. Atomic waker pattern (double-check) — subtle but important for correct async I/O
